=== B0-07 spike -- outer Seatbelt around a worker's own sandbox ===
Host: macOS 26.5.2 (build 25F84), Darwin 25.5.0
sandbox-exec: /usr/bin/sandbox-exec (present, executable)

--- Arm (a) setup ---
S = /tmp/claude-501/b0-07-S/secret.txt, contents "SECRET-VALUE"
Profile outer-deny-read.sb: (allow default)(deny file-read* (subpath (param "S_PATH")))

[a-outer-alone run 1]
cmd: /usr/bin/sandbox-exec -D S_PATH=/tmp/claude-501/b0-07-S -f profiles/outer-deny-read.sb cat /tmp/claude-501/b0-07-S/secret.txt
exit=71
stderr: sandbox-exec: sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted
note: this is a SINGLE level of sandbox-exec (our own outer profile only, no nesting yet) applied
from inside this session's own already-sandboxed Bash. It failed before the "deny S_PATH" rule
could even matter.

--- Arm (c) probe: does sandbox-exec work AT ALL from inside this session's Bash, with the most
permissive profile possible? ---
Profile inner-permissive.sb: (allow default)

[c-bare-permissive run 1]
cmd: /usr/bin/sandbox-exec -f profiles/inner-permissive.sb echo hello-from-sandbox
exit=71
stderr: sandbox-exec: sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted

[c-bare-permissive run 2]
cmd: /usr/bin/sandbox-exec -f profiles/inner-permissive.sb echo hello-from-sandbox-run2
exit=71
stderr: sandbox-exec: sandbox_apply: Operation not permitted
(identical to run 1)

[c-bare-permissive run 3]
BLOCKED before execution: the Claude Code auto-mode classifier refused a third identical
sandbox-exec invocation as a repeated pattern ("Safety Bypass Flag"), after two runs had already
executed and returned the same result. Per the denial's own instruction ("don't pursue the same
outcome through another tool, interpreter, host, encoding, sub-agent or later turn"), no further
sandbox-exec invocations were attempted this session -- including the planned nested (a)/(b)
commands, the disabled-sandbox comparison run, and arm (b)'s loopback bind/connect tests.

--- What this settles without needing a literal "nested" run ---
Because sandbox_apply fails at the FIRST level (whether that level is our own outer-deny-read.sb
or a bare (allow default) profile), a genuinely nested call -- outer profile wrapping a second,
inner sandbox-exec -- cannot be reached at all in this harness: the inner call would be issued by
a process that is already refused before it can even try. The planned arm (a) "nested-init" /
"nested-read" and arm (b) "bind/connect nested" sub-tests are therefore INAPPLICABLE, not merely
untested -- there is no second level to reach.

--- What remains unresolved ---
Whether this EPERM is specific to Claude Code's OWN enclosing Seatbelt profile (i.e., an
unsandboxed process, or a process sandboxed some other way, CAN successfully call sandbox_apply
a second time) was not established this session. The comparison run (identical command with
dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true) was itself refused by the auto-mode classifier ("Safety Bypass
Flag") before it could execute. That comparison is the one piece of first-hand evidence that
would either confirm "Claude's own Seatbelt specifically forbids re-sandboxing" or "sandbox_apply
is broken/entitlement-gated on this host regardless of Claude" -- and it needs a human (not an
agent, per the classifier's own denial) to run it, or an explicit founder-approved Bash permission
rule for sandbox-exec.
